@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Altering a View";
}

<div class="container-fluid">

    <!-- =========================
         PAGE HEADER
    ========================== -->

    <div class="mb-4">

        <h1 class="fw-bold">
            Altering a View
        </h1>

        <p class="text-muted">
            Learn how to modify an existing SQL Server View using
            the ALTER VIEW statement with practical examples.
        </p>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         INTRODUCTION
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                What is ALTER VIEW?
            </h2>

            <p>
                The <strong>ALTER VIEW</strong> statement is used to modify
                the definition of an existing SQL Server View.
            </p>

            <p>
                When the structure or SELECT query of a View needs to be
                changed, we can use ALTER VIEW instead of dropping the
                View and creating it again.
            </p>

            <p>
                The ALTER VIEW statement changes the View definition while
                keeping the View object in the database.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         BASIC SYNTAX
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                ALTER VIEW Syntax
            </h2>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>ALTER VIEW ViewName
AS
SELECT column1, column2
FROM TableName
WHERE condition;</code></pre>

            <h5 class="fw-bold mt-4">
                Syntax Explanation
            </h5>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    <strong>ALTER VIEW</strong> specifies that an
                    existing View will be modified.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    <strong>ViewName</strong> is the name of the existing View.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    <strong>AS</strong> separates the View name from
                    the SELECT query.
                </li>

                <li>
                    The SELECT statement defines the new View definition.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         CREATE ORIGINAL VIEW
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Step 1: Create an Existing View
            </h2>

            <p>
                First, suppose we already have a View named
                <strong>EmployeeDetailsView</strong>.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>CREATE VIEW EmployeeDetailsView
AS
SELECT
    EmployeeId,
    EmployeeName,
    Department
FROM Employees;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                This View currently displays EmployeeId, EmployeeName,
                and Department.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         VIEW DATA
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Checking the Existing View
            </h2>

            <p>
                We can retrieve the data from the View using SELECT:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT *
FROM EmployeeDetailsView;</code></pre>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         ALTER VIEW - ADD COLUMN
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example 1: Adding a Column
            </h2>

            <p>
                Suppose we now want to include the employee's salary
                in the View.
            </p>

            <p>
                We can modify the existing View using ALTER VIEW:
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>ALTER VIEW EmployeeDetailsView
AS
SELECT
    EmployeeId,
    EmployeeName,
    Department,
    Salary
FROM Employees;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The View now returns the Salary column along with the
                previously selected columns.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         ALTER VIEW - WHERE
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example 2: Adding a WHERE Condition
            </h2>

            <p>
                We can also change the View so that it returns only
                employees from the IT department.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>ALTER VIEW EmployeeDetailsView
AS
SELECT
    EmployeeId,
    EmployeeName,
    Department,
    Salary
FROM Employees
WHERE Department = 'IT';</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                After altering the View, only IT department employees
                will be returned.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         ALTER VIEW - CHANGE QUERY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Example 3: Changing the View Query
            </h2>

            <p>
                The SELECT query inside an existing View can be changed
                completely using ALTER VIEW.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>ALTER VIEW EmployeeDetailsView
AS
SELECT
    EmployeeName,
    Salary
FROM Employees
WHERE Salary &gt; 50000;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The View now displays only EmployeeName and Salary for
                employees whose salary is greater than 50000.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         VERIFY ALTERED VIEW
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Verifying the Altered View
            </h2>

            <p>
                After using ALTER VIEW, execute the View to verify
                the new definition.
            </p>

            <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>SELECT *
FROM EmployeeDetailsView;</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3">
                The result will now be based on the new SELECT query
                defined by ALTER VIEW.
            </p>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         ALTER VIEW VS CREATE VIEW
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                CREATE VIEW vs ALTER VIEW
            </h2>

            <div class="table-responsive">

                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped">

                    <thead class="table-primary">

                        <tr>
                            <th>CREATE VIEW</th>
                            <th>ALTER VIEW</th>
                        </tr>

                    </thead>

                    <tbody>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                Creates a new View.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Modifies an existing View.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                Used when the View does not exist.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Used when the View already exists.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                Creates the View object.
                            </td>

                            <td>
                                Changes the definition of the existing
                                View object.
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                    </tbody>

                </table>

            </div>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         IMPORTANT POINTS
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-warning shadow-sm mb-4">

        <h3 class="fw-bold mb-3">
            Important Points
        </h3>

        <ul class="mb-0">

            <li class="mb-2">
                ALTER VIEW is used for an existing View.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                It changes the SELECT definition of the View.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                The View name remains the same.
            </li>

            <li class="mb-2">
                The underlying tables are not automatically changed
                just because the View definition is altered.
            </li>

            <li>
                Always test the altered View after making changes.
            </li>

        </ul>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         BEST PRACTICES
    ========================== -->

    <div class="card shadow-sm mb-4">

        <div class="card-body">

            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">
                Best Practices
            </h2>

            <ul>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Check the existing View definition before altering it.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Test the new SELECT query separately before using
                    ALTER VIEW.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Make sure applications depending on the View are
                    not affected by unexpected column changes.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use meaningful and consistent View names.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Verify the View after altering it.
                </li>

            </ul>

        </div>

    </div>


    <!-- =========================
         SUMMARY
    ========================== -->

    <div class="alert alert-primary shadow-sm mb-4">

        <h2 class="fw-bold">
            Summary
        </h2>

        <p class="mb-0">
            The ALTER VIEW statement is used to modify the definition
            of an existing SQL Server View. We can change the SELECT
            query, add or remove selected columns, and modify filtering
            conditions without dropping the View object first.
        </p>

    </div>


